      <h1 style="font-size:1.8rem;line-height:1.2;">Floor Jack Safety: 5 Things Nobody Told Me Before I Started Working Under My Car</h1>
    </div>

    <p>I bought my first floor jack to change my own oil. I watched a YouTube video, slid under the car, and got to work. It was not until later that I realized how many things I did wrong that could have killed me.</p>

    <p>I am not trying to scare you. I just wish someone had told me these 5 things before I started.</p>

    <h2>1. Never Get Under a Car Supported Only by a Floor Jack</h2>

    <p>This is the biggest one. Floor jacks have hydraulic seals that can fail. Not "might fail" but statistically will fail eventually. A jack is for lifting, not supporting. You need jack stands under the car before you go under it.</p>

    <p>I did not use jack stands my first 3 oil changes. I was lucky. Do not rely on luck.</p>

    <h2>2. Jack Stands Go on the Frame, Not the Control Arm</h2>

    <p>Even with jack stands, placement matters. You want the stands under the frame rail or the designated jacking points on your car. Putting them on suspension components or control arms means the car can shift when you start working on it.</p>

    <p>Check your owner manual for the exact jacking points. Every car has them. Use them.</p>

    <h2>3. Chock the Wheels That Stay on the Ground</h2>

    <p>When you lift the front of the car, the rear wheels can still roll. A car rolling off jack stands is a nightmare scenario. Put wheel chocks behind the wheels that stay on the ground. A $10 set of chocks is cheap insurance.</p>

    <h2>4. Jack on Flat Ground Only</h2>

    <p>I learned this one when my jack slipped on a slight incline. The car shifted about 6 inches before the jack stands caught it. That was enough to make me never do it again. If you have to work on a slope, position the car so the jack is on the high side and the stands are on level ground.</p>

    <h2>5. Check Your Jack Capacity</h2>

    <p>A 1.5 ton jack lifts 3,000 lbs. Most sedans weigh 3,000-4,000 lbs. So a 1.5 ton jack is barely enough for half the car. A 2 ton jack gives you more margin. A 3 ton jack is overkill for a sedan but nice to have if you also work on trucks.</p>

    <p>I use a 3 ton Daytona jack now. It lifts my sedan in 2 pumps and I never worry about capacity.</p>

